The '''Tata Nano''' is a [[city car]]/[[microcar]] manufactured and marketed by Indian automaker [[Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles|Tata Motors]] from 2008 to 2018. It was primarily sold in India as an inexpensive [[economy car]] for motorcycle and scooter riders &mdash; with a launch price of {{INR|100,000}} ({{USD|2500}}) on 10 January 2008.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2008/01/10/tata-nano-the-worlds-cheapest-car/?_r=0|title=Tata Nano: The World's Cheapest Car|first=Richard S.|last=Chang|date=10 January 2008 }}</ref> The Nano has a [[rear-engine, rear-wheel-drive layout]] and was initially produced as a four-door [[saloon car]] with no rear cargo opening, with a five-door [[hatchback]] version introduced in 2015.

Tata Motors projected production figures of 250,000 annually at launch. This was not achieved, and various factors led to a decline in sales volume, including delays during the factory relocation from [[Singur]] to [[Sanand]], early instances of the Nano catching fire and the perception that the Nano was unsafe and lacked quality from its aggressive cost cutting. Actual sales reached 7,591 for model year 2016-2017. The project lost money, as confirmed by former Tata Sons chairman [[Cyrus Mistry]] and by 2017 Tata Motors management.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://pixr8.com/tata-motors-says-nano-investment-significantly-written-off/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170119022652/http://pixr8.com/tata-motors-says-nano-investment-significantly-written-off/|archive-date=19 January 2017|title=Tata Motors Shuts Down Loss Making Small Car Tata Nano Production|website=Pixr8}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.hindustantimes.com/business-news/nano-a-loss-making-product-says-tata-motors/story-8RqBVdKfbjCo4Y1TH2fQPI.html|title=Nano a loss-making product, says Tata Motors|date=4 November 2016|newspaper=Hindustan Times}}</ref>

In 2017, Tata Motors said manufacturing would continue due to the company's emotional commitment to the project.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://overdrive.in/news-cars-auto/tata-motors-to-continue-tata-nano-production-for-now/|title=Tata Motors to continue Tata Nano production for now|date=27 September 2017|website=Overdrive}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thehansindia.com/posts/index/Amaravati-Tab/2017-09-29/Tata-Nano-sales-to-continue-in-spite-of-no-demand/329860|title=Tata Nano sales to continue in spite of no demand|date=29 September 2017|website=The Hans India}}</ref> Production was eventually halted in May 2018.<ref name="wire1">{{cite news |title=Air Asia Case Puts Spotlight Once Again on Cyrus Mistry's 'Legacy Hotspots' |url=https://thewire.in/business/air-asia-tata-cyrus-mistry |access-date=5 June 2018 | publisher=The Wire}}</ref> The [[Sanand Plant (Tata Motors)|Sanand Plant]] subsequently manufactured other hatchbacks, including the [[Tata Tiago|Tiago]] and [[Tata Tigor|Tigor]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tatamotors.com/press/tata-motors-sanand-facility-reaches-100-capacity-utilization-to-meet-growing-demand-for-tiago-and-tigor/|title=Tata Motors' Sanand facility reaches 100% utilization|publisher=Tata Motors|date=7 August 2018|accessdate=13 November 2021|location=Ahmedabad}}</ref>

==History==
After successfully launching the low cost [[Tata Ace]] truck in 2005, Tata Motors began development of an affordable passenger car that would appeal to the many Indians who ride motorcycles.<ref name="yahoonano">{{cite news | url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/pf_article_102865.html | title=The Next People Car | work=Forbes | date=19 April 2007 | agency=Yahoo! – ABC News Network | access-date=6 June 2012 | author=Meredith, Robyn | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120719081851/http://finance.yahoo.com/news/pf_article_102865.html | archive-date=19 July 2012 | df=dmy-all }}</ref> The purchase price of this [[no frills]] auto was brought down by dispensing with most nonessential features, reducing the amount of steel used in its [[construction]], and relying on low cost Indian labour.{{citation needed|date=June 2012}}. The design featured only a driver's side wing mirror, one wiper blade, only three lug nuts per wheel and a fuel tank without filler cap.<ref name="bsmotor">{{cite news |author=Bijoy |first=Kumar Y. |date=12 April 2009 |title=Tata Nano – Nano second to none! |url=http://www.bsmotoring.com/storypage.php?autono1002 |access-date=6 June 2012 |work=Business Standard}}</ref> Tata said it was the first time a 2-cylinder petrol engine was being used in a car with single balancer shaft.

At introduction, the Nano received attention due to its low price of 1 lakh rupees (Rs. 100,000).<ref name=spiegel>{{cite news | url=https://www.spiegel.de/international/world/bodacious-tata-india-delivers-world-s-cheapest-car-a-528106.html | title=Bodacious Tata: India Delivers World's Cheapest Car | work=Spiegel Online International | date=11 January 2008 | access-date=6 June 2012}}</ref>  Some promotional material labeled the vehicle as "The People’s Car".<ref>{{cite web |date=2008-01-10 |title=Tata Motors unveils the People's Car |url=https://www.greencarcongress.com/2008/01/tata-motors-unv.html |access-date=2024-03-04 |website=Green Car Congress}}</ref>



===Expectations and effects===
Expectations created for the car during the run up to its production were out of proportion with realised sales. A 2008 study by Indian rating agency CRISIL projected the Nano would expand the nation's car market by 65%,<ref name="economictimes1">{{cite news|url=http://economictimes.indiatimes.com/articleshow/2694186.cms |title=Tata Nano may expand market by 65%: CRISIL- Automobiles-Auto-News By Industry-News |work=The Economic Times |date=12 January 2008 |access-date=8 June 2009}}</ref> but, as of late 2012, news reports have detailed the underwhelming response of the Indian consumer to the offering; sales in the first two fiscal years after the car's unveiling remained steady at about 70,000 units. Tata intended to maintain a capacity to produce the car in much larger quantities, some 250,000 per year, should the need arise.<ref>{{cite web |author=Nair |first=Avinsh |date=26 April 2012 |title=Two years on, Tata Nano sales yet to hit top gear |url=http://www.indianexpress.com/news/two-years-on-tata-nano-sales-yet-to-hit-top-gear/941736/0 |access-date=29 October 2012 |work=[[The Indian Express]]}}</ref>

It was anticipated that its 2009 debut would greatly affect the used car market, and prices did drop 25–30% prior to the launch.<ref>{{cite news|title=Tata Nano sends used car prices tumbling in India|url=http://www.themotorreport.com.au/3656/tata-nano-sends-used-car-prices-tumbling-in-india|access-date=30 July 2011|newspaper=The Motor Report|date=11 February 2008}}</ref> Sales of the Nano's nearest competitor, the [[Maruti 800]], fell by 20% immediately following the unveiling of the Nano.<ref name="Tankedmarket">{{cite web |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/News/NewsArticle.aspx?AR=231051 |title=Nanomania overwhelms Indian car market |work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] |date=7 February 2008 |access-date=10 February 2008}}</ref> It is unknown if the Nano has had a lasting effect on the prices of and demand for [[substitute good|close substitutes]]. In July 2012, Tata's Group chairman Ratan Tata, who retired in same year, said that the car had immense potential in the developing world while admitting that early opportunities were wasted due to initial problems.<ref>{{cite news| url= http://profit.ndtv.com/News/Article/why-ratan-tata-thinks-nano-has-enormous-potential--307830?pfrom=home-otherstories| title=Why Ratan Tata thinks Nano has 'enormous potential' | date= 13 July 2012}}</ref> Due to the sales drops, only a single unit was produced in June 2018. The [[Sanand Plant (Tata Motors)|Sanand Plant]] now produces other hatchbacks like [[Tata Tiago|Tiago]] and [[Tata Tigor|Tigor]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tatamotors.com/press/tata-motors-sanand-facility-reaches-100-capacity-utilization-to-meet-growing-demand-for-tiago-and-tigor/|title=Tata Motors' Sanand facility reaches 100% utilization|publisher=Tata Motors|date=7 August 2018|accessdate=13 November 2021|location=Ahmedabad}}</ref>

===Singur factory pullout===
{{Main|Tata Nano Singur controversy}}

Tata Motors announced in 2006 that the Nano  would be manufactured in [[Singur]], [[West Bengal]].<ref name="economist"/> Local farmers soon began protesting the 'supposed' forced acquisition of their land the new factory entailed.<ref name="economist">{{cite news | url=https://www.economist.com/business/2008/08/28/nano-wars | title=Nano wars: Tata threatens to make the world's cheapest car somewhere else | newspaper=The Economist | date=28 August 2008 | access-date=6 June 2012}}</ref> Tata first delayed the Nano launch and later decided to build the car in a different state, [[Gujarat]], instead.<ref>For initial delay, see [http://inhome.rediff.com/money/2007/aug/03tata.htm Rain, political unrest delay Tatas' dream car] Rediff.com, 3 August 2007
* For pullout from West Bengal site, see {{cite news | url=http://www.ndtv.com/convergence/ndtv/StoryPrint.aspx?ID=NEWEN20080067550&ch=633588296281093750 | title=It's final: Tata Motors to pull out of Singur | publisher=NDTV.com | date=3 October 2008 | agency=NDTV Convergence Limited | access-date=6 June 2012 }}
* For relocation to Gujarat, see {{cite web | url=http://www.tatamotors.com/know-us/manufacturing.php | title=Manufacturing: Sanand | work=Tata Motors | access-date=6 June 2012 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120920004648/http://tatamotors.com/know-us/manufacturing.php | archive-date=20 September 2012 | df=dmy-all }}</ref>

==Price==
[[File:Tata Nano Bangalore 2709.JPG|thumb|Rear]]
Announced as the lowest priced [[production car]] in the world, Tata aimed for a price of one [[lakh]] [[Indian rupee|rupees]], or {{Indian Rupee}}100,000, which was approximately $2,000 US at launch time.<ref>{{cite news |author=Kurczewski |first=Nick |date=23 March 2009 |title=Tata Nano Launched in Mumbai |url=http://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2009/03/23/tata-nano-launched-in-mumbai/ |access-date=6 June 2012 |work=[[The New York Times]] |series=Wheels Blog}}</ref> Only the very first customers were able to purchase the car at that price, and as of 2017, the price for the basic Nano started around {{Indian Rupee}} 215,000 ($3,400 US).<ref>For only first customers receiving {{Indian Rupee}} 100,000, see {{cite news | url=https://www.reuters.com/article/tatamotors-nano-idUSBOM1678720090716 | title=Tata Motors to deliver first Nano on Friday | date=16 July 2009 | agency=Thomson Reuters | access-date=6 June 2012 | first=Janaki | last=Krishnan}} Many Indians were of the opinion that Tata just managed to offer offered a four-wheeled [[auto-rickshaw]] for a price of INR 2,00,000.
* For 2017 price, see {{cite web | url=http://nano.tatamotors.com/price-list.php | title=Price List: Select to view citywise ex-showroom price of the Nano. | work=Tata Motors | access-date=10 January 2017}}</ref> Increasing material costs may be to blame for this rapid rise in price.<ref name="motorauthority2">{{cite web |author=Ireson |first=Nelson |date=5 August 2008 |title=Rising costs could eat Tata Nano's profits |url=http://www.motorauthority.com/news/hatchbacks/rising-costs-could-eat-tata-nanos-profits/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081202142340/http://www.motorauthority.com/news/hatchbacks/rising-costs-could-eat-tata-nanos-profits/ |archive-date=2 December 2008 |access-date=6 August 2008 |publisher=Motor Authority}}</ref>

Compared to the [[Volkswagen Beetle]] it had a relatively low price, though still high in terms of the average salary of an Indian industrial worker or farmer.{{citation needed|date=June 2025}} In 1990, a Beetle from Mexican factories was priced at $5,300,<ref>{{cite news |author=Uhlig |first=Mark |date=20 October 1990 |title=Mexico City Journal; Miss the VW Bug? It Lives Beyond the Rio Grande |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/10/20/world/mexico-city-journal-miss-the-vw-bug-it-lives-beyond-the-rio-grande.html?pagewanted=all&src=pm |access-date=8 August 2012 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> about ${{Formatnum: {{Inflation|US|5300|1990}}}} in 2022. The [[Ford Model T]]'s initial price was about $850, equivalent to ${{Formatnum: {{Inflation|US|850|1909}}}} in 2022. The price of the Nano was only just higher<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2012 |title=National Geographic - Megafactories - Season 1, Episode "Tato Nano" |url=https://www.natgeotv.com/za/shows/natgeo/megafactories |access-date=2024-03-04 |website=www.natgeotv.com |language=en |quote=Sir Ratan Tata is a man on a mission: to make India's roads safer by getting people off motorbikes and into the world's cheapest car - the Tata Nano. In India it's common to see a family of four ride together on a motorbike. On bad roads in Monsoon rain that's a lethal way to commute. Tata envisages the Nano as a 'people's car' - India's answer to the Model T. But to bring his car to the people he must overcome some formidable challenges. Design issues and skyrocketing costs threaten to blow out the car's $2200 price tag, and violent protests force him to dismantle and shift his 3 billion dollar Megafactory 2100km across India.}}</ref> than the corrected price of the [[Smith Flyer|Briggs & Stratton Flyer]] of the 1910s, with the Flyer costing US$125 ($1,767 in 2016){{citation needed|date=June 2025}}, while the Flyer might be classified as more of a [[go-kart]] than a car.{{clarification needed|date=June 2025}}

===Cost-cutting features===
The Nano's design implements many measures to reduce manufacturing costs.
{| class="wikitable"
! Tata Nano
|-
|The [[Trunk (automobile)|trunk]] was only initially accessible from inside, as the rearmost panel did not open.<ref name=green>{{cite web |url=http://ca.autoblog.com/2010/09/15/quick-spin-2011-tata-nano-cx-driving-the-worlds-cheapest-car/ |title=Quick Spin: 2011 Tata Nano CX – Driving the world's cheapest car |publisher=Autoblog Canada |date=15 September 2010 |access-date=29 July 2011 |archive-date=28 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120328084715/http://ca.autoblog.com/2010/09/15/quick-spin-2011-tata-nano-cx-driving-the-worlds-cheapest-car/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> (full hatchback offered in 2015).
