== Tata GenX Nano (2015–2018) ==
[[File:Tata Nano - GenX 01.JPG|thumb|right|2015 Tata GenX Nano (facelift)]]
In May 2015, to revive the model's sales, Tata Motors introduced a series of enhancements both inside and outside the car. The name was changed to ''GenX Nano'' to underline the changes, the range was offered in 21 different versions, among them a 5-speed automated manual transmission.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.alvolante.it/news/tata-nano-genx-2015-341195 |title=Tata Nano GenX: cambio di rotta (in Italian) [Tata GenX Nano: change of direction] |publisher=[[Al Volante]]|date=19 May 2015 |access-date=20 July 2018}}</ref> Electric power steering, air conditioning, and Bluetooth radio were available on top models in addition to new colors and alloy wheels. The body was strengthened and the front and rear bumpers were changed, which slightly increased the length to 3,164 millimeters. The interior was also redesigned with new fabrics and improved soundproofing. The rear hatchback and 5-seat homologation were introduced. The engine remained the 2-cylinder {{convert|624|cc|cuin|lk=on|abbr=on}} with 38&nbsp;hp.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.tatamotors.com/press/tata-motors-launches-the-genx-nano-range-its-new-compact-hatch/ |title=Tata Motors launches the GenX Nano range, its new compact hatch |publisher=[[Tata Motors]]|date=19 May 2015 |access-date=19 July 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://carandbike.com/news/2015-tata-genx-nano-launched-prices-start-at-rs-1-99-lakh-764296 |title=2015 Tata GenX Nano Launched; Prices Start at 1.99 Lakh |publisher=CarAndBike.com |date=20 May 2015 |access-date=19 July 2018}}</ref>

==Alternative power plants==
The Nano was driven by a petrol engine. Though several variants were proposed, none were put into production. An upscale version was shown at an auto show.

===Compressed-air engine===
[[Tata Motors]] signed an agreement in 2007 with a French firm, [[Motor Development International]], to produce a [[compressed air car]] Nano.<ref name=range/> While the vehicle was supposed to be able to travel approximately {{convert|200|km}} on $3 US of electricity to compress the air, Tata's Vice-President of Engineering Systems confirmed in late 2009 that vehicle range continued to be a problem.<ref name=range>[http://www.dnaindia.com/money/report_tamo-s-ambitious-air-car-faces-starting-trouble_1316093 TaMo’s ambitious ‘Air Car’ faces starting trouble] ''Daily News and Analysis'', 25 November 2009.</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Hall |first=Kenneth |url=http://www.motorauthority.com/blog/1023575_tata-nano-could-come-with-optional-air-powered-engine |title=Tata Nano could come with optional air-powered engine |publisher=MotorAuthority |date=10 July 2008 |access-date=29 August 2010}}</ref>

===Electric vehicle===
Tata discussed the possibility of producing an electric version,<ref name=mdie>{{cite web |url=http://www.domainb.com/companies/companies_t/Tata_Motors/20080820_tata_motors.html |title=Tata plans E-Nano, electric version of Rs 1-lakh car |publisher=domain-b.com |date=20 August 2008 |access-date=8 June 2009}}</ref> and while it showcased an [[electric vehicle]] Nano at the 2010 [[Geneva Motor Show]],<ref>{{cite news |url=http://content.usatoday.com/communities/driveon/post/2010/03/worlds-cheapest-ev-tata-nano-electrifies-geneva-show/1 |work=USA Today |title=World's cheapest EV: Tata Nano electrifies Geneva show |date=4 March 2010}}</ref> no such car came onto the market.

If an EV Nano had been produced the expectations were that it would have been a highly affordable [[electric car]]<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.merinews.com/catFull.jsp?title=Let%20Tata%27s%20Nano%20be%20electric&articleID=141391 |title=Let Tata's Nano be electric |publisher=Merinews.com |access-date=8 June 2009 }}{{Dead link|date=November 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> using [[lithium-ion]] batteries and having a range of {{convert|80|mi}}.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://content.usatoday.com/communities/driveon/post/2010/03/worlds-cheapest-ev-tata-nano-electrifies-geneva-show/1 |title=World's cheapest EV: Tata Nano electrifies Geneva show&nbsp;— Drive On: A conversation about the cars and trucks we drive |work=USA Today |date=4 March 2010 |access-date=29 August 2010}}</ref> A Norwegian electric car specialist, [[Miljø Grenland|Miljøbil Grenland AS]], was named as a proposed partner in the project.<ref name=mdie/>

There was, however, an electric version of the Nano released for fleet operators like Ola by Jayem Automotives which was called the Jayem Neo EV, launched in 2020. It was discontinued in 2019-2020 and only 400 were made.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jayem Neo – Tata Nano EV to be launched on Nov 28, 2017 |url=https://www.team-bhp.com/forum/electric-cars/192726-jayem-neo-tata-nano-ev-launched-nov-28-2017-a-4.html |access-date=2026-03-23 |website=Team-BHP.com |language=en}}</ref>

===Tata Nano CNG emax (Bi-fuel)===
The ''Nano CNG emax'' was launched in October 2013. It could run on either gasoline or compressed natural gas. Initially, it was to be sold in Delhi and parts of Maharashtra and Lucknow, where CNG was available at fuel outlets.<ref name="WSJ1008213">[https://online.wsj.com/article/DN-CO-20131008-004342.html "Tata Motors Introduces Bi-Fuel Version of Nano Minicar"]. ''The Wall Street Journal'', Santanu Choudhury</ref> The bifuel engine was powered by both CNG and petrol. In natural gas operation, the engine delivered maximum power of 33 horsepower and 45 Nm of maximum torque. The tank had a capacity of 32 liters (8 US Gal/7 UK Gal) of CNG.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tata.com/article/inside/TSWoO7vEjxs=/TLYVr3YPkMU= |title=Tata Motors launches the Tata Nano CNG emax |publisher=[[Tata Motors]]|date=8 October 2013 |access-date=28 July 2018}}</ref>

=== Tata Super Nano ===
In December 2014, Coimbatore-based JA Motorsport presented a 230&nbsp;hp 1.3-liter engined version of the Nano called the 'Super Nano' at the Autocar Performance Show. Featuring a full body kit, slick tyres, a bolt-on roll cage, and smoked head and tail lamps, the Super Nano featured carbon fibre components, Recaro seats, and steering-mounted paddle shifters.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://indianautosblog.com/2014/12/super-tata-nano-ja-motorsport-161583|title=230hp Super Tata Nano Revealed, Costs INR 25 Lakhs|website=indianautosblog.com|date=12 December 2014 |access-date=2016-03-28}}</ref>

==European export==
[[File:Nano Tata.jpg|thumb|The Tata Nano Europa [[concept car]]]]
An [[luxury good|upscale]] Nano [[concept car]] called Nano Europa was shown at the 2009 [[Geneva Motor Show]].<ref name=europe>{{cite web|url=http://www.autonews.com/article/20090304/ANE02/903039952/1164|title=Tata unveils a Nano for Europe|work=Automotive News|access-date=18 September 2014}}</ref> However, there was no progress towards producing or marketing this upscale specification.

==Car fires==
There were reports of several fire incidents involving the Nano.<ref name=timeslede/> The company denied those were connected to the car's design or its parts and blamed "foreign electrical equipment" found on top of the exhaust system.<ref name=timeslede/> The company offered to retrofit the exhaust and electrical systems but refused to recall the cars.<ref name=timeslede/> Tata extended the warranty on the car, including those already sold, from 18 months to four years in early December 2010.<ref name=timeslede>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/12/10/business/global/10tata.html?pagewanted=1&_r=1|title=Tata's Nano, the Car That Few Want to Buy|work=The New York Times |date=10 December 2010 |access-date=18 September 2014 |last1=Bajaj |first1=Vikas }}</ref>

==Reception==
The Nano received a mixed reception from Indian consumers; reasons given included that it was still too expensive compared to a motorcycle, and the extended waiting time for delivery (a few months).<ref name="defence.pk">{{cite web|url=http://www.defence.pk/forums/world-affairs/164315-where-did-all-go-wrong-tata-s-nano.html|title=Where Did It All Go Wrong For Tata's Nano?|work=Pakistan Defence|access-date=18 September 2014|archive-date=1 October 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131001123543/http://www.defence.pk/forums/world-affairs/164315-where-did-all-go-wrong-tata-s-nano.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Although it was identified as the most affordable car,<ref>{{cite web |author=Tavares |first=Avinash |title=Marketing lessons from tata nano |url=http://www.slideshare.net/avinash2701/marketing-lessons-from-tata-nano |access-date=18 September 2014 |website=[[Slideshare]]}}</ref> a more expensive secondhand car signaled higher social status;  the Nano was considered a "[[Poverty in India|poor man]]'s" vehicle, turning some customers away.<ref>{{cite news| title=Cheap proves costly for Tata Nano in status-conscious India | date=30 January 2012 | work=Daily News|location=New York | agency=Agence France-Presse | url-access=limited | url=https://www.nydailynews.com/2012/01/30/cheap-proves-costly-for-tata-nano-in-status-conscious-india/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230926072314/https://www.nydailynews.com/2012/01/30/cheap-proves-costly-for-tata-nano-in-status-conscious-india/ | url-status=live | archive-date=26 September 2023 | access-date=4 February 2014 }}</ref> The fires and other safety issues were also a concern.<ref name="defence.pk"/>

Tata Motors ended FY16 selling 21,012 Nanos, up from 16,901 in FY15.<ref>{{cite web |author=Das |first=Sohini |date=2017-04-15 |title=Is the Tata Nano in the last leg of its journey? |url=http://www.business-standard.com/article/companies/is-the-tata-nano-in-the-last-leg-of-its-journey-117041400542_1.html |url-access=subscription |access-date=15 April 2017 |website=Business Standard}}</ref>

=== Safety===
{{Global NCAP
|year=2014
|region=India
|description=Tata Nano – No Airbags
|reference_code=Tata-Nano-NO-Airbags.pdf
|adult_stars=0.00
|adult_points=0.00
|child_stars=0.00
|child_points=0.00
}}
In 2014, [[Global NCAP]] 1.0 crash-tested an Indian-market Tata Nano at the [[ADAC]] facility in Germany in its pilot round of 64km/h front offset deformable barrier crash tests for its Safer Cars for India project (based on [[Latin NCAP]] 2013). They also crashed the Nano in the configuration for the UN's R94 test. Tata had made it public that a version of the Nano designed for export to Europe had passed R94, and they were also expecting a four star [[Euro NCAP]] result.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Tata Nano crash test on video |url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/motoring/tata-nano-crash-test-video |access-date=2022-03-23 |website=Autocar |language=en}}</ref> In contrast, the Indian car failed the R94 test and scored zero stars for adult and child occupant protection in [[Global NCAP]]'s tests. The Nano was not fitted with airbags, not even one for the driver, nor [[Anti-lock braking system|ABS]]. During the 64km/h NCAP test the passenger compartment not only became unstable but also showed extremely high levels of intrusion of the steering column and waist-level intrusion of the A-pillar, showing high risk of life-threatening injury to the driver's head, neck and chest and also indicating that less critical body regions like the knees, tibias and feet were unlikely to be recoverable. The Nano had only static two-point seatbelts for the rear seats and no child seat available for sale in India could pass the installation check, meaning that the Nano was unsuitable for safely transporting children. It achieved zero stars for child occupant protection, scoring no points in all areas of assessment.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2014/jan/31/tata-nano-safety-crash-test-results |title=Tata Nano safety under scrutiny after dire crash test results|work=Guardian|date=31 January 2014 |access-date=9 September 2017 |last1=Oltermann |first1=Philip |last2=McClanahan |first2=Paige }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/video/2014/jan/31/tata-nano-fails-crash-test-video |title=Tata Nano falls short of global crash test standards – video|work=Guardian|date=31 January 2014 |access-date=9 September 2017}}</ref>

==Sales==
At the time of launch Tata Motors planned to sell 250,000 units per year. The maximum sales ever achieved was 74,527 units during FY 2011-2012 and then sales declined rapidly year after year leading to a negligible market share of the car in the "A" segment. The product was expected to be phased out soon as dealers stopped placing orders.<ref>{{cite news |date= 26 November 2017|title= Tata Nano likely to be phased out soon as dealers stop placing orders|url= https://www.dnaindia.com/automobile/report-tata-nano-likely-to-be-phased-out-soon-as-dealers-stop-placing-orders-2562571|work= DNA India|access-date= 14 October 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date= 26 November 2017|title= Bidding farewell to 'cheapest car': Tata Nano likely to be phased out soon|url= https://www.timesnownews.com/business-economy/companies/article/bidding-farewell-to-cheapest-car-tata-nano-likely-to-be-phased-out-soon/133295|work= TimesNowNews|access-date= 14 October 2018}}</ref>

FY 2009–2010 30,000 approx<ref name=IndExpress2012>{{cite news |last= Nair|first= Avinash|date= 26 April 2012|title= Two years on, Tata Nano sales yet to hit top gear|url= http://archive.indianexpress.com/news/two-years-on-tata-nano-sales-yet-to-hit-top-gear/941736/|work= The Indian Express|location= Ahmedabad|access-date= 14 October 2018}}</ref>

FY 2010–2011 70,432<ref name=IndExpress2012 />

FY 2011–2012 74,527<ref name=IndExpress2012 />

FY 2012–2013 53,848<ref>{{cite web |date=2013-05-05 |title=Popularity continues to elude Nano, sales down 88% |url=http://www.hindustantimes.com/Autos/Latest-News/Popularity-continues-to-elude-Nano-sales-down-88/Article1-1055088.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130507083248/http://www.hindustantimes.com/Autos/Latest-News/Popularity-continues-to-elude-Nano-sales-down-88/Article1-1055088.aspx |archive-date=7 May 2013 |access-date=18 September 2014 |work=Hindustan Times}}</ref>

FY 2013–2014 21,129<ref>{{cite web|url=http://indianexpress.com/article/business/business-others/sales-of-tata-nano-worlds-cheapest-car-set-to-hit-six-year-lows/|title=Sales of Tata Nano, world's cheapest car, set to hit six-year lows|date=5 March 2015|work=The Indian Express}}</ref>

FY 2014–2015 16,903<ref>{{cite web|last1=Aggarwal|first1=Yogita|title=7 Reasons Why Tata Nano Did Not Take Off as Expected|url=http://www.letuspublish.com/7-reasons-tata-nano-not-take-off-expected/|website=letuspublish.com|access-date=18 March 2016|date=1 March 2014}}</ref>

FY 2016-2017 7,591<ref>{{cite news |last= Kumar|first= Arjun|date= 25 April 2017|title= Tata Nano Sales At An All Time Low, 174 Units Sold In March|url= https://www.motorbeam.com/tata-nano-sales-numbers-all-time-low/|work= MotorBeam|access-date= 14 October 2018}}</ref>

FY 2017-2018 April - October 1,502

==End of production== 
Due to the low sales of the model (only one Nano was assembled in June 2018 against the 275 assembled in June 2017) Tata Motors announced the end of production without any direct successor. The Nano was not accepted by potential buyers and sales were always lower than projected.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/view/articles/2018-07-11/rip-nano-india-s-car-market-is-no-place-for-cheap-thrills |title=RIP Nano. World's Cheapest Car Goes Up in Smoke |publisher=[[Bloomberg L.P.|Bloomberg.com]]|date=11 July 2018 |access-date=19 July 2018 |url-access=subscription}}</ref>
The [[Sanand Plant (Tata Motors)|Sanand Plant]] now produces other hatchbacks like the [[Tata Tiago|Tiago]] and the [[Tata Tigor|Tigor]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.tatamotors.com/press/tata-motors-sanand-facility-reaches-100-capacity-utilization-to-meet-growing-demand-for-tiago-and-tigor/|title=Tata Motors' Sanand facility reaches 100% utilization|publisher=Tata Motors|date=7 August 2018|accessdate=13 November 2021|location=Ahmedabad}}</ref>

==Awards==
* 2010 Business Standard Motoring Indian car of the year<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.business-standard.com/india/news/bs-motoring-jury-award-2010-tata-nano/380711/ | title=BS Motoring Jury Award 2010: Tata Nano | work=Business Standard | date=26 December 2009 | access-date=6 June 2012}}</ref>
* 2010 Bloomberg UTV-Autocar car of the year<ref>{{cite web |author= |date=18 January 2010 |title=Nano, Pulsar among winners of Bloomberg UTV-Autocar awards |url=http://www.financialexpress.com/news/Nano--Pulsar-among-winners-of-Bloomberg-UTV-Autocar-awards/568379/ |access-date=22 February 2010 |work=The Financial Express}}</ref>
* 2010 Edison Awards, first place in the transportation category<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.edisonawards.com/Winners2010.php |title=Edison Awards 2010 honoring innovation in the development and launch of new products and services |publisher=Edisonawards.com |date=2 April 2010 |access-date=17 October 2010 |archive-date=8 October 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101008214026/http://www.edisonawards.com/Winners2010.php |url-status=dead }}</ref>
* 2010 [[Good Design Award (Chicago)|Good Design Awards]], in the category of transportation<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rediff.com/business/slide-show/slide-show-1-auto-tata-nano-shines-wins-global-design-award/20101223.htm |title=Tata Nano shines! Wins global design award |work=Rediff.com |date=5 January 2010 |access-date=23 December 2010}}</ref>
* 2014 India's Most Trusted hatchback car, according to The Brand Trust Report 2014 edition<ref>{{Cite web|url = http://www.trustadvisory.info/mab-14/Automobile.htm|title = The Brand Trust Report 2014|website = Trust Research Advisory}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url = http://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/tata-nano-again-tops-most-trusted-automobile-list-report/article5702001.ece|title = Tata Nano again tops 'most trusted automobile' list |date = 2014-02-18|work = The Hindu Business Line |access-date = 2015-12-24}}</ref>

